  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/7e881b5cb3613ff72264739b1cbe1214c1041a78d201cfc323476910e7ce8e3c.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(4,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(2,4)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(2)?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $(2,4)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=4.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [1,5]$ is $1$, hence, ${f}^{\u2032}(4)=1.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
